WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.330
Film Courage: Are you able to tell us how
you found financing for DANGER ONE?

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.750
Tom Oesch, Filmmaker: It was pretty straightforward.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:15.890
Once we had the sizzle done…let me rewind.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:23.470
When Stephen the writer and I started working
on the script and the sizzle, I had lunch

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:30.080
with a producer friend of mine and I just
briefly gave him the log line and he’s like

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.080
“Oh my gosh!

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.080
That’s awesome.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.220
When you’re done, let me see it.”

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.960
We’re good friends and go back a few years.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.430
Also someone I met through a friend that I
went to school with obviously.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:48.420
But when we were all done my managers sent
it out to people and a few companies and I

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:54.879
sent it to my producer friend and he loved
it.

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:57.679
How did that go?

00:00:57.679 --> 00:01:02.999
Because he had produced the pyramid for FOX.

00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:10.470
So he was in Lebanon at the time that I sent
him the stuff because it was the Middle Eastern

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.100
premiere of the PYRAMID.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.609
And at the same time he was there another
movie called POCKET LISTING also premiered

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:19.880
in Lebanon.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.579
That one was produced by the people who ended
up financing DANGER ONE.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:31.890
And so Chatty (my producer friend) was in
Lebanon at the time he was set to go to the

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.450
premiere of POCKET LISTING.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.039
And at time he met the financiers and they
had just finished a movie and they were looking

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:44.259
for something new and I told them what they
were looking for and Chatty was like “Oh

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:45.259
my gosh.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:46.259
You should check this out.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:48.920
I’ve got a sizzle and a script.”

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.979
At that point things moved pretty fast.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:59.880
We probably spent two months rewriting the
script and five months later we were on set.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:05.249
Film Courage: So you put your own money in
for the sizzle reel and in the end you got

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:06.770
someone to finance the film.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.750
Tom: Yes I wouldn’t be able to finance the
film.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:14.240
On an editor’s salary I would be able to
finance a film this size.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.080
Our budget wasn’t high especially for a
movie this ambitious and it was a full union

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.160
shoot but we got it done.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:31.440
Film Courage: You think because it was mostly
interior or in that one warehouse or whatever

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.740
with the ambulance that this saved so much
money?

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:35.760
Tom: Oh yes.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.460
I mean honestly I don’t know how we got
this done with the time we had and the budget,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.540
it’s a bit of a miracle.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.820
And in hindsight I do think it would be good
if we had (and I know every director will

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.980
tell you that) a little bit more.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:59.450
But we still had a whole lot of locations
(we had to cover 23 days) so for us to be

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:08.430
able to spend the first two weeks of those
23 days in that warehouse basically covering

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:14.010
everything that happens in the warehouse and
also inside the ambulance was the only way

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.370
to get that done.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:21.180
The last two weeks that’s when we headed
out but we still had so many locations where

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.620
you have a company move in the middle of the
day doing one scene here and then everyone

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.620
gets in their cars and their trucks and you
travel somewhere else.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:38.120
But a lot of the scenes we sort of shot around
that warehouse.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:43.280
The funny thing is there is a scene in the
movie where they are getting rid of the firefighter’s

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:49.090
in the manhole and we were looking for a location
for that.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.320
So when we were in the warehouse.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.870
We used it as a sound stage and it should
not be used as a sound stage.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.770
Sound stages are sound proof, that’s what
makes them a sound stage.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:07.900
This was actually an old warehouse but what’s
worse is literally right next to that warehouse

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.190
was a recycling plant.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:14.400
So we’re shooting inside this warehouse
and all night long/all day long you’ve got

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.730
these trucks pushing containers with metal
cans and bottles against our wall which was

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.750
why a lot of the sound was unusable and we
had to ADR a lot of it.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.260
But we were like “Can we take a look at
this recycling plant?

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.690
Maybe we can shoot there?”

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.040
And we eventually ended up shooting a whole
scene in there literally just next door.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:41.130
Film Courage: So when you found out that most
of the sound wasn’t usable how shocking

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.130
was this?

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:43.130
Maybe you knew that was coming?

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:47.310
Tom: Well we knew it was coming because we
heard it on set.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:52.060
This was not a good situation to be in, especially
on a low-budget film you don’t want to have

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.380
to ADR half your movie.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.920
A lot of the stuff that happened in there
we had to ADR in post.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.139
We had a long ADR schedule on this movie.

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:04.280
Film Courage: How long did that set you back?

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:10.200
Because you probably had an idea that it was
going to be released by a certain time.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.889
Tom: Just because certain things didn’t
go according to planned during the production

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:20.639
(this happens on movies a lot), the post production
budget kind of moved into production.

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:25.590
So when you get to post production you have
to be really smart.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:29.350
Things will just take longer because there
now is less money to get it done than there

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:30.480
was before.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.590
So our post…how long was that?

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:49.220
Because we shot in 2016 in March and April
and I were we in post for like a year…probably?

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:55.380
Because also actors are out of town shooting
things and you have to start scheduling around

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.980
their availability.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:03.790
The further removed from principal photography
the harder it gets.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:15.010
We finished toward the fall of 2017, so yes
post was a year.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:20.530
Film Courage: Did you have all the actors
come back for ADR?

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:21.700
All the major players?

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.700
Tom: I think so?

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.900
Tom Everrett Scott was in for like 4 days.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.110
Yes Dennis O’Hare we had to come in from
New York.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.340
We even had some ADR done in Lebanon because
the actor was in Lebanon.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:41.310
So we had to visit a studio there and do a
Skype call.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:48.610
So yes, pretty much all the major players
were in for a day (half a day) and some of

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:49.610
the smaller ones.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:50.870
Film Courage: How does this work?

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:54.770
So you’ve gone through the entire movie
and you know from this time mark to this the

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.830
sound is horrible and this line needs to be
redone.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.690
So you’re charting all of this?

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:04.090
Tom: I wasn’t but the post sound department
they were.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.600
And that was the first time I’d done this.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.120
They basically just gave me a long list of
here’s the time code, here’s the line,

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.350
get the line.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:17.480
The good thing about this is it actually allows
you to tweak the performance.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:22.220
I know a lot of actors don’t like ADR and
I get it because you’re in a booth and it

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.890
doesn’t feel…you know being on set there
is the environment and the other actor is

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:31.550
in front of you and you can really get into
it, you’re having an actual interaction.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:37.570
It allows you to get out of your head and
really just be in that moment.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:46.190
ADR you are in tiny padded booth by yourself
and the acting experience becomes very artificial.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:48.320
You’re just saying the line over and over
again.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:50.990
Because a lot of actors hate doing it.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:57.020
As a director and for the post crew it allowed
us to tweak certain performances to even out

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.430
the tone a little bit, to take certain things
down a notch.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:06.240
Also to be writing certain likes that were
actually off camera because scenes might have

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:07.240
gotten cut.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.790
There are a couple of scenes that may not
have even shot.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:21.430
We some how had to get that content in where
ADR was the best.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:28.800
So it wasn’t just replacing the crappy production
audio it was also replacing the story.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:38.689
Film Courage: So how do you get that to match
the bad sound that’s in Vernon [where they

00:08:38.689 --> 00:08:42.519
shot the film] that probably has a very distinct
feel and then you have this pristine both?

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:44.459
How is that working?

00:08:44.459 --> 00:08:54.120
Tom: During the mix there is an ADR editor
so they already do some filtering on it and

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.119
then during the mix you try to make it sound
as much as possible as during production.

00:08:59.119 --> 00:09:03.720
Sometimes they just advise you to ADR the
whole scene because they are just like we’re

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.470
never going to be able to match this.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.980
If you know what lines are ADR you can tell.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.660
When I watch it I’m like “there’s an
ADR right there.”

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:19.069
I don’t know if audiences can, but it’s
not easy.

00:09:19.069 --> 00:09:24.730
Film Courage: So you know it because you know
there is more reverb or whatever, you can

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.300
just hear more of an echo or something and
this was too crisp but most of the time the

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.300
audience won’t know.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:34.399
Tom: Well now having done it I can actually
hear it in other movies, even on like big

00:09:34.399 --> 00:09:36.899
movies it’s like “There’s an ADR line
right there.”

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:39.670
Film Courage: You have an editor’s ear.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:45.529
Tom: Well in reality TV, I guess we do some
of that but it’s not on this level.

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:52.029
ADR in reality TV is talent will say it in
their iPhone and record it and then like text

00:09:52.029 --> 00:09:53.110
it over.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.170
It’s not quite on the same level as on a
feature.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:01.540
Film Courage: But then too with a lot of reality
TV there is music playing the entire time

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:09.389
under the [dialogue] so that would probably
filter a lot.

00:10:09.389 --> 00:10:18.559
Tom: But reality TV you do quite a bit of
wild nights with dialogue cheated that people

00:10:18.559 --> 00:10:23.249
record on their iPhones during interviews
and we make it sound like it’s actually

00:10:23.249 --> 00:10:24.839
happening.

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:27.290
Like I said, not a lot of reality in reality
TV.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:30.760
Film Courage: iPhone audio can sound excellent.

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:36.709
Tom: Well audio on reality TV is already kind
of crappy so the audio from the iPhone is

00:10:36.709 --> 00:10:39.529
going to fit in nice (it’s not going to
stand out that much).

